<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maratha Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marathas, Maratha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maratha Empire was an Indian state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> in the seventeenth century in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Its long war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> drained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, and in the eighteenth century the Marathas became the dominant power in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Defeat at Panipat in 1761 and wars with the British led to its final collapse in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maratha-empire.jpg" alt="Maratha Empire" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India1760_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Marathas were a Marathi-speaking warrior group from the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>. Shivaji, a Maratha aristocrat of the Bhonsle clan, began resistance to the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> in 1645 by taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-fort">fort of Torna</a>. He established Hindavi Swarajya, meaning the self-rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> as his capital, and was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a>, or sovereign, in 1674. At his death his dominion held about 300 forts, about 40,000 cavalry and naval establishments on the west coast. His son Sambhaji succeeded in 1681, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> headed south the same year with his whole court and an army of about 500,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death in 1707, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s grandson Shahu revived Maratha power and gave much authority to the Bhat family, who became hereditary peshwas, or prime ministers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> from 1720, is credited with expanding the kingdom tenfold by 1740. He defeated the Nizam at Palkhed on 28 February 1728 and at Bhopal in 1737. After Shahu died in 1749 the peshwas became the effective rulers.</p><p>The leading families, the Scindia, Holkar, Bhonsle and Gaekwad, extended conquests in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and grew more independent. The expansion was halted by the defeat at Panipat in 1761 at the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>, but the Marathas retook most of their lost territory ten years later under Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhavrao-i">Madhavrao I</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a> defeated him in 1802, Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a> sought protection from the British East India Company, whose intervention in the Second and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> destroyed the confederacy by 1818.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The state was a confederacy of four rulers under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> (Pune): the Scindia, the Gaekwad at Baroda, the Holkar at Indore and the Bhonsle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>. Historians question both empire and confederacy as names: Barbara Ramusack notes that neither is fully accurate, and Stewart Gordon observes that power within the leadership changed as often as every decade. Many rulers paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a>, a regular tax, to the Marathas.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> created the Ashtapradhan, a council of eight ministers headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>. Under the peshwas his homogeneous army gave way to a feudal force provided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">sardars</a>, with soldiers of many backgrounds, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and Europeans. The navy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhoji-angre">Kanhoji Angre</a> built dockyards along the western coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maratha Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As the Mughal Empire dissolved and governors in Bengal and the Deccan asserted their autonomy, and as the <b>Maratha Empire</b> rose, the rulers of Oudh gradually affirmed their own sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the <b>Maratha</b> commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern Karnataka plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of Gingee in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Mysore wars (1766–1799) and the Anglo-<b>Maratha</b> wars (1772–1818) left the Company in control of much of India south of the Sutlej, and after the Marathas' defeat no native power threatened it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Marathas</b> under Raghunathrao drove Timur Shah from Lahore, Multan and Kashmir in 1757, Ahmad declared a jihad, gathering warriors of many Afghan tribes and the Baloch under Mir Nasir Khan I, Khan of Kalat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The state that emerged was large but under constant pressure from the <b>Marathas</b>, and its boundaries shifted repeatedly during the eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mysore also fought the <b>Marathas</b> and the Nizam of Hyderabad, who became allies of the British in the Third and Fourth Anglo-Mysore Wars that destroyed the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
